Multiple data points show poor start to year for Taiwan's economy - Xinhua News Agency
Xinhua Taipei, February 11 (Xinhua reporters Zhao Bo and Huang Yang) Exports fell for five consecutive months, the manufacturing purchasing managers' index (PMI) contracted for seven consecutive months, and the consumer price index (CPI) hit "three-digit" again... In recent days, Taiwan's authorities have successively released economic data for January 2023.
Experts say that Taiwan's economy is underperforming in the new year, and growth pressures are mounting.
According to the Taiwan authorities' Ministry of Finance, Taiwan's exports in January this year amounted to US$31.51 billion, down 21.2% year-on-year and for the fifth consecutive month in negative growth.
The export value of the 11 major export categories has declined compared to the same period last year.
Rubber and plastics, as well as their products, and optical equipment saw the steepest declines, with year-over-year reductions of 37.6% and 35.7%, respectively.
Electronic component exports fell 20.1% year-on-year, marking the largest decline in 11 years.
Among them, integrated circuits declined 18.3% year-on-year, marking the largest drop in 14 years.
The department's head of statistics, Domina, said that this shows that the weakness of consumer electronics demand is spreading, and the electronics industry is entering a period of correction.
Statistics show that Taiwan's imports and exports both declined in January, with a trade surplus of just $2.34 billion, the lowest level in nearly three years.
The Taiwanese government's financial affairs authority believes that the global demand for terminals is declining, industry adjustments to inventory suppress the willingness of suppliers to buy, the export momentum is unlikely to improve in the short term, and Taiwan is facing a recession in exports in the first quarter of this year that is higher than expected.
According to the Central Institute of Economic Management in Taiwan, the seasonally adjusted Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) for Taiwan's manufacturing sector in January this year was 40.4%, down 3.3 percentage points from December last year and contracting for seven consecutive months.
In the economic monitoring index system, PMI has 50% as its boundary line; an index greater than 50% indicates expansionary conditions, while an index below 50% indicates contraction.
In January, five components of Taiwan's PMI contracted in new orders, production, and employment, while supplier delivery times fell and inventories contracted.
All six major industries are reporting PMI contraction, with contraction speeds in descending order as follows: electrical and mechanical equipment industry (35.1%), electronic and optical industry (35.2%), chemical and biopharmaceutical healthcare industry (39.2%), basic materials industry (42.4%), transportation equipment industry (42.6%), and food and textile industry (45.7%).
Regarding prices, Taiwan’s “Statistics Bureau” reported that the year-on-year increase in CPI for January was 3.04%, once again breaching the 3% threshold since July of last year.
Among them, vegetable prices jumped 28.25%, reaching their highest level in 17 months.
Taiwan’s Statistics Bureau specialist Cao Zhihong openly acknowledged that the price hikes for vegetables indeed exceeded expectations. Excluding the factors of the Spring Festival, the average CPI for January and February this year should be close to 2.71% from last December. Inflation pressure still exists and is at high levels.
Looking ahead to Taiwan’s economy this year, experts express pessimism based on reports from local media such as The United Daily News and Yahoo Finance.
Professor Liu Daeng, of the Department of Economics at Taipei Central University, analyzed that a recession in exports could cause cash flow problems for export manufacturers and an increase in the risk of debt default.
At the same time, reduced orders lead to firms cutting labor costs, which affects the labor market and household economies, thereby impacting consumption capacity.
A researcher at the Taiwan Economic Research Institute (Taiwan Institute of Economics) said inflation in Europe and the United States is unresolved this year, demand continues to shrink, and Taiwan's export recession is expected to continue into the first half of the year.
Due to Taiwan’s economy being supported by exports, its growth for the year is expected to be weaker than last year due to sluggish export performance.
Newly-appointed central committee members, alternate members, and main-level officials at provincial and ministerial levels complete the learning seminar on implementing Xi Jinping Thought on Socialism with Chinese Characteristics for a New Era and the 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China - Rolling News - Hunan Daily - Huasheng Online
The newly elected members of the Politburo Standing Committee and alternate members of the Central Committee as well as leading cadres at provincial and ministerial levels have completed their training on learning and implementing Xi Jinping Thought on Socialism with Chinese Characteristics for a New Era and the spirit of the 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China.
February 11, 2023 13:07:20 [Source: Xinhua News Agency] [Editor: Li Xufeng] Font size: [Large Medium Small]
Xinhua News Agency, Beijing, February 10 – The seminar for new members of the Central Committee, alternate members, and principal leading cadres at or above the ministerial level to study and implement Xi Jinping’s Thought on Socialism with Chinese Characteristics for a New Era and the spirit of the 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China concluded late yesterday afternoon at the Central Party School (National Academy of Governance). Central Political Bureau member and Secretary of the Central Secretariat Cai Qi attended the closing ceremony and delivered a summary speech.
He stated that to thoroughly study and implement President Xi Jinping’s important speech at the opening ceremony of the seminar, one must further strengthen support for “two establishments” and resolutely uphold “two maintenances,” enhance their abilities, and work diligently in practice. They should strive to advance all aspects of China’s modernization in an all-round way, achieving accomplishments that can withstand the test of history, practice, and the people in the process of comprehensively building a modern socialist society and comprehensively advancing the great rejuvenation of the Chinese nation.
During the seminar, all participants guided by Xi Jinping's Thought on Socialism with Chinese Characteristics for a New Era, studied in depth the spirit of the 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China and President Xi Jinping's opening remarks at the seminar's start. They engaged in in-depth discussions and exchanges based on actual circumstances, deepening their understanding of a series of major theoretical and practical issues concerning China's modernization. This strengthened their confidence and determination to advance the great rejuvenation of the Chinese nation under the strong leadership of the Central Committee with Comrade Xi Jinping at its core. It also heightened their sense of responsibility and mission to perform their duties, do their work well, and ensure a good start to the new era and new journey.
At the graduation ceremony, 10 representatives from the seminar spoke, reporting on their learning gains and exchanging experiences.
Zaicai stated that learning and implementing Xi Jinping’s new era of socialism with Chinese characteristics and the spirit of the 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China is both a primary political task in the current and future period and a long-term strategic task. It should be carried out according to the requirement of “comprehensive study, comprehensive grasp, and comprehensive implementation,” so as to push forward the work of learning and implementing the spirit continuously deeper, more realistically, and closer to people's hearts.
To more deeply understand the decisive significance of “two establishments,” to uphold Comrade Xi Jinping’s core position and the authority of the Central Committee of the Party and its centralized and unified leadership resolutely, and to continuously improve one’s political judgment, political insight, and political execution capabilities, always maintaining high consistency in terms of ideology, politics, and action with the Central Committee of the Party with Comrade Xi Jinping at its core.
We must strive to master this vision of Marxist theory, consciously use the Party's innovative theories to arm our minds, guide our practices, advance our work, work hard in deep learning, insight, and diligence, to truly learn firm beliefs, to learn absolute loyalty, and to learn mission responsibilities.
To implement in a resolute and dynamic manner the various decisions and deployments of the 20th Congress of the Communist Party of China, to advance solidly the Chinese-style modernization, to pull up our sleeves and work hard, to lower our bodies and serve as construction managers, and to turn the blueprint into reality step by step.
It is imperative to remember that comprehensive and strict governance of the Party must be pursued at all times, and self-revolution within the Party must also continue forever. The political responsibility for comprehensive and strict governance of the Party should be pressed firmly, with party political construction serving as the overarching principle, and efforts should be made to advance the new great project of Party building in the new era.
To enhance capabilities in promoting high-quality development, serving the people, and managing risks, we should foster a style of investigation and research, and resolutely prevent and overcome formalism and bureaucratism.
Shi Tiefeng, Li Ganjie, Li Shuei, Chen Wenqing, Liu JinGuo and Wang Xiaohong attended the graduation ceremony, and Chen Xi presided over it.
VNA Hanoi – VNA correspondent for Vietnam’s Prime Minister Nguyen Xuan Phuc and his wife have successfully completed their official visit to Singapore and Brunei, achieving comprehensive, practical, and specific results.
Over the course of three days of his visit to Singapore and Brunei, Premier Pham Minh Chinh attended more than 30 different activities.
The achievements inject new momentum into deepening, broadening, and enhancing the strategic partnership between Vietnam and Laos, as well as the comprehensive partnership between the two countries.
During his visit to the two countries, Prime Minister Pham Minh Chinh continued to reaffirm Vietnam’s and the Party’s foreign policy line, which is independence, diversity, flexibility, willingness to be good friends and reliable partners for all countries, and responsible members of the international community.
Strengthening relations with Singapore and Brunei is each country’s need, aimed at enhancing the well-being of its people, and committed to peace, stability, and development in the region and the world.
At meetings and negotiations, Prime Minister Van Minh and the leaders of Singapore and Brunei unanimously agreed to continue strengthening political mutual trust with Vietnam through high-level delegation visits, effective implementation of bilateral cooperation mechanisms and important cooperation agreements.
Many cooperation documents were signed during this visit.
Among them, the Memorandum of Understanding on a Green and Digital Economy Partnership signed with Singapore and the Action Plan for Strengthening Comprehensive Partnership between Vietnam and Brunei from 2023 to 2027 are highlights.
Vietnam and Singapore have signed a Memorandum of Understanding on Green and Digital Economy Partnership.
Image from VNA (Vietnam News Agency)
During meetings with business leaders, seminars, or attending economic forums, Prime Minister Pham Minh Chinh emphasized that Vietnam welcomes and creates favorable conditions for expanding investment scopes in areas such as digital transformation, green energy, high-tech, and innovation and creativity by Singapore and Brunei enterprises in Vietnam.
This visit not only helps to deepen cooperation between Vietnam and Singapore and Brunei in traditional areas such as politics, diplomacy, economy, security, defense, education and training, oil and gas, tourism, people-to-people exchanges, but also creates frameworks for cooperation in new potential areas such as digital economy, green economy, energy transition, innovation and creativity, smart cities, and sustainable development.
King Hassanal Bolkiah of Brunei and Prime Minister Fan Ming are preparing for a bilateral symposium.
Image from VNA
During his visits to both countries, Prime Minister Pham Minh Chinh made time to visit and listen to the concerns and aspirations of the Vietnamese community residing there.
In their meeting between the two leaders, Prime Minister Pham Minh Minister suggested that both governments pay more attention to ensuring fair treatment for the local Vietnamese community, provide them with opportunities to contribute to their societies, and create favorable conditions for fostering relations between the two countries.
All of these suggestions were agreed to and supported by both countries' leaders.
In particular, the Prime Minister also shared with the leaders of the two countries the desire for an ASEAN Community of unity, peace, stability and prosperity; unanimously agreed to maintain cooperation in solidarity with ASEAN countries and enhance ASEAN's central role in the complex and changing context of regional and international situations.
With the significant achievements achieved, Prime Minister Van Minh's official visit to Singapore and Brunei has been instrumental in the effective implementation of the 13th Foreign Route as a priority for strengthening relations with ASEAN neighbours, in the effective implementation of the 12th Conclusion of the Political Bureau of the Communist Party of Vietnam (CPC) on the new situation in Vietnam, and in contributing positively to the strengthening of national unity.
(Complete)
What exactly was "the object" that was shot down over Alaska?
Mystified and divided by speculations.
The U.S. military on Friday afternoon (February 10) again used America's most advanced stealth jet F-22 to shoot down an unidentified object flying over the U.S. state of Alaska.
The location of the hit was ten miles from Alaska's coast.
This is the second time in less than a week that US forces have downed an airborne object.
Last Saturday, a spy balloon from China drifted over US airspace for several days before being destroyed near the coast of South Carolina in the United States.
The Pentagon said the second object to be shot down entered U.S. airspace for the first time on Thursday night and was tracked by the U.S. military as it moved over northeastern Alaska, flying at an altitude of 12,000 meters (40,000 feet), about the size of a small car and much smaller than the Chinese spy balloon that was shot down earlier.
The Pentagon also believes that there is currently no indication that the object is manoeuvrable.
White House National Security Council Strategic Communications Coordinator John Coby said on Friday that while the exact appearance of the object in high-altitude flight is unclear, it poses a reasonable threat to the safety of civil aviation aircraft.
Kobe stated that the object is different from the spy balloon discovered in the United States, which did not have a payload hanging from it.
Kobe said, “We call it an ‘object’ because it is the best description we can make at this point.”
President Biden listened to the Pentagon's suggestions before ordering the shooting down of the object, and he made the decision very carefully, Kobe added.
The official description of this object by the US authorities can be summarized as follows: about the size of a small car, without control sticks, without suspension systems, and flying at an altitude of around 10,000 meters.
But these pieces of information still fail to answer fundamental questions, such as what this object is, which country it belongs to, and what task it is performing.
Currently, many analyses and speculations online suggest that this could be another Chinese balloon.
It may have come from China, as did the spy balloon that invaded US airspace and the Chinese balloon that flew over Latin America, which performed some task in different locations.
Some have linked this "object" from Alaska to unidentified flying objects (UFOs).
A report in Newsweek quotes a tweet from Peter Meijer, a former US Representative, who said on Friday, "I wish this 'unidentified flying object' was actually a spy balloon, rather than a legitimate UFO."
The irony of conflating extraterrestrial spacecraft with weather balloons is striking, but it does not warrant an interstellar conflict, he said.
Meager tweeted this shortly after Kobe confirmed that the object in Alaska had been shot down.
The Weekly Times reports that it is not just Major who suspects the object to be a UFO.
Republican Representative Tim Burchett of Tennessee criticized the government's cover-up of intelligence last month, and is demanding that the Office of the Director of National Intelligence (DNI) release reports on "unidentified flying objects."
Iran Commemorates Revolution Anniversary as Hackers Sabotage President's TV Speeches in Support of Protests
The Islamic Republic of Iran held an official gathering on Saturday to commemorate the 44th anniversary of the Iranian Revolution, but anti-government hackers briefly interrupted President Ebrahim Raisi's televised speech.
The President’s televised address was interrupted on the internet for about a minute, with a banner of hackers opposed to the Iranian government appearing on the screen. They go by the name “Edalat Ali (Ali Justice).”
Another voice shouted “Death to the Islamic Republic.”
Reuters today reports that Iran is celebrating its revolution anniversary and hackers have disrupted national TV broadcasts.
As Iran officially commemorates the 44th anniversary of the Iranian Revolution, Lacey's hardline government faces the toughest challenge of the young protesters demanding their resignation, but Lacey still calls on the deceived youths to repent so they can be pardoned by Iran's supreme leader.
Protests swept across Iran following the death of 22-year-old Mahsa Amini, who died in custody by the country's morality police in September.
Iranian security forces carried out a deadly crackdown on protests, which is one of the strongest challenges the Islamic Republic has faced since the revolution in 1979 that overthrew its 2500-year-old monarchy.
As part of the amnesty to commemorate the anniversary of the revolution, the Iranian authorities released on Friday the imprisoned dissident Fahd Mesami and the Iranian-French scholar Fariba Adelha, who were on hunger strike.
Earlier, Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei issued a decree of amnesty that includes some prisoners arrested in recent anti-government protests.
According to the human rights organization HRANA, as of Friday, 528 protesters have been killed, including 71 minors.
The report says that 70 government security personnel were killed.
It is believed that up to 19,763 protesters were arrested.
For weeks, Iranian leaders and official media have been calling for voting at Saturday’s rally to show opposition to the protests and support for the government.
On Friday night, on the eve of the anniversary of the Iranian Revolution, the official media showed fireworks as part of a government-sponsored celebration, and people chanted "Allahu Akbar!"
(God is the greatest!)
However, one can also hear many people shouting "Death to the dictator!" at the rally.
And death of the Islamic Republic in videos posted on social media.
On Saturday, government television broadcast live footage of official rallies across the states.
In Tehran, as part of the official celebration, national anti-ballistic missiles, drones, anti-submarine cruisers and other military equipment were displayed.
President Lacey's speeches are often interrupted by the slogan "dead in the United States" - an iconic slogan in state assemblies.
The crowd still shouted "Death to Israel."
Reuters said that Adelkhah, who has been in prison since 2019, is one of seven French nationals detained in Iran, which is a factor in the deterioration of relations between Paris and Tehran over the past few months.
She was sentenced to five years in prison for national security charges in 2020.
They later confined her at home, but she returned to prison in January.
Adelkhah denies these charges.
And just a week before Mesa was released, supporters warned that he faced a risk of death due to his hunger strike.
He was arrested in 2018 for protesting mandatory headscarf wearing.
The French Foreign Ministry on Friday responded to Adelkhah's release and called for her freedom to be restored, including allowing her to return to France if she wished.
